## Root-cause analysis: D-16 shared scope-exit rotation equivocation

### Exact bug location

**File:** `crates/fuse/src/content_ops.rs`, function `publish_file_node`, the first-publish branch at **line 272**:

```rust
// content_ops.rs:272
if is_first_publish {
    // First publish: no prior record → seq 1, expected None, TEE enroll.
    ...
    let record = cipherbox_core::ipns::create_ipns_record(&ipns_key_arr, &value, 1, 86_400_000)  // seq HARDCODED to 1
    ...
    let req = cipherbox_api_client::IpnsPublishRequest {
        ...
        expected_sequence_number: None,   // ← first-publish path, no CAS
    };
    match cipherbox_api_client::ipns::publish_ipns(api, &req)
        .await
        .map_err(|e| e.to_string())?      // ← the API 400 surfaces HERE
    ...
} else {
    // Update publish: CAS via crate::metadata::publish_with_cas_retry (resolves seq, bumps to seq+1)
}
```

The error string `"Per-file node publish failed for ino N: ..."` is logged at `crates/fuse/src/read_ops.rs:912` (and the Windows twin at `crates/fuse/src/platform/windows/write_ops.rs:1034`), wrapping the `Err` returned by this function.

### Why seq=1 is emitted (the actual mechanism)

`publish_file_node` is called **only** from the file `release` path (`read_ops.rs:894`). The `is_first_publish` argument is `is_new_file`, computed at `read_ops.rs:699`:

```rust
let is_new_file = handle.temp_path.is_some() && {
    fs.inodes.get(ino).map(|i| match &i.kind {
        InodeKind::File { cid, .. } => cid.is_empty(),   // ← the whole determinant
        _ => false,
    }).unwrap_or(false)
};
```

So a release publishes at **seq 1** whenever the file inode's `cid` is empty. The inode `cid` is only filled by the `UploadComplete` event drain, and that drain is **guarded by `write_generation`** (`fs.rs:461-465`):

```rust
if inode.write_generation == result.write_generation {
    if let InodeKind::File { ref mut cid, .. } = inode.kind { *cid = result.new_cid.clone(); }
}
```

If that completion is dropped as stale (a superseding write bumps `write_generation`, or a FUSE-T deferred/duplicated `release`), the inode `cid` stays **empty**, so a subsequent release again computes `is_first_publish=true`. Critically, each first-publish call re-seals the file node with a fresh `now_ms` (`content_ops.rs` sets `created_at`/`modified_at = now_ms`), producing a **different node-envelope CID every time**. Two seq-1 publishes with different CIDs is exactly what the Phase 71 anti-equivocation gate (`apps/api/src/ipns/ipns.service.ts:345`, commit 703bc0083) correctly rejects with 400: `stored=cidA, incoming=cidB, sequence=1`.

The scope-exit rotation itself is **not** the emitter — the SDK rotation engine (`crates/sdk/src/rotation/engine.rs`, via `FuseRotationDeps`) resolves each node and CASes at `resolved.sequence_number` correctly. The equivocating publish is the file's own release-path re-publish, which the rotation window's extra mount activity (nudges, relink, sync) makes reliably observable in D-16.

### The existing correct CAS path to reuse

The `else` branch already routes through `crate::metadata::publish_with_cas_retry`, which **resolves the current sequence and bumps** (`metadata.rs:48-61`):

```rust
let seq = coordinator.resolve_sequence(api, ipns_name).await?;   // existing record → 1
let new_seq = seq.checked_add(1)...;                             // → 2
... expected_sequence_number: Some(seq.to_string()) ...          // CAS at seq 1 → publishes seq 2, no equivocation
```

The replay path already solves the identical problem with a resolve-first idempotency guard: `crates/fuse/src/replay.rs:324` (`publish_child_node` resolves before its seq-1 publish and skips/handles when a record already exists).

### Minimal patch proposal (Rust-only, single shared function)

`publish_file_node` in `content_ops.rs` is the **single** per-file publish entry point for both fuser (macOS/Linux) and WinFsp (`operations.rs:95` and `platform/windows/operations.rs:244` both re-export this same function), so one edit fixes all three platforms. Guard the first-publish tail with an authoritative resolve; take the seq-1 + TEE-enroll path only when the record is genuinely absent, otherwise fall through to the existing sequence-bumping CAS branch.

Insert immediately **before line 272** (`let value = format!("/ipfs/{}", node_cid);` is at line ~268, just above), and change `if is_first_publish {` → `if do_first_publish {`:

```rust
    // `is_first_publish` is the caller's release-time HINT (the file inode had an
    // empty CID, read_ops.rs:699-706). A dropped UploadComplete (write_generation
    // mismatch, fs.rs:461) or a duplicated/deferred FUSE-T release can leave that
    // hint set AFTER the per-file record already exists at seq 1. Re-running the
    // seq-1 tail re-seals a FRESH node envelope (now_ms changes each call → new CID),
    // which the API anti-equivocation gate (Phase 71 D-05) rejects with 400. Confirm
    // the record is genuinely absent before taking the first-publish (seq 1 + TEE
    // enroll) tail; if it already resolves, route the re-publish through the
    // sequence-bumping CAS path instead (mirrors replay.rs:324 publish_child_node).
    let do_first_publish = if is_first_publish {
        match crate::publish::resolve_ipns_for_replay(coordinator, api, file_ipns_name).await {
            crate::error::IpnsResolveOutcome::NotFound => true,
            crate::error::IpnsResolveOutcome::Found(_) => false,
            crate::error::IpnsResolveOutcome::Error(e) => {
                return Err(format!(
                    "resolve before per-file first-publish for {file_ipns_name}: {e} \
                     — refusing to risk a seq-1 equivocation"
                ));
            }
        }
    } else {
        false
    };

    if do_first_publish {
        // ... existing first-publish branch unchanged (seq 1, expected None, TEE enroll) ...
    } else {
        // ... existing CAS branch unchanged (publish_with_cas_retry) ...
    }
```

**Why this and not relaxing the gate / not always-CAS:** The genuine first-publish branch must stay reachable because it performs the TEE enrollment (`encrypted_ipns_for_tee` + `key_epoch`, crypto rule #7) that `publish_with_cas_retry` does not send, and the strict gate requires the first publish to embed seq=1. The resolve guard preserves TEE enrollment for real new files while deterministically diverting any stale-hint re-publish onto CAS.

**Signature/threading:** none. `resolve_ipns_for_replay` is `pub(crate)` (`publish.rs:39`), `IpnsResolveOutcome` is `pub` (`error.rs:8`); both are already in-crate. `coordinator` and `api` are already in scope. No caller changes; `is_first_publish` stays in the signature (still the hint input).

**Scope:** Rust-only. No TS/bin helper changes. `pnpm api:generate` not needed (no API surface change).

### Local validation

- **Compile:** `cargo check -p cipherbox-fuse --features fuse` (macOS/Linux) — the `#[cfg(any(feature = "fuse", feature = "winfsp"))]` gate means the WinFsp variant is compile-checked only on the CI `Cargo Check & Test (Windows)` job (Windows is CI-only per project memory; budget a CI round-trip to confirm the shared function still builds under `winfsp`).
- **No unit coverage:** `publish_file_node` has no live-API unit test; `cargo test` will not exercise the equivocation. The authoritative check is the D-16 desktop-e2e leg `tests/desktop-e2e/scripts/shared-scope-exit-rotation.mts` (a `.mts` requiring the local stack: docker infra + `api dev` + a freshly built desktop mount with matching `TEST_LOGIN_SECRET`). Run it end-to-end and confirm no `Per-file node publish failed ... equivocation` in the mount log and that Part A still passes the single-rotation `+1` sequence-bump assertion. Note the ROADMAP still lists `70.1-13-PLAN.md` (the D-16 real-mount acceptance leg) as open — this fix is a prerequisite for that leg to go green.

